WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.470
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Emily Broad Lieb, who is a clinical professor

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:10.759
of law and faculty director for Harvard Law
School's food law and Policy Clinic.

00:00:10.759 --> 00:00:12.230
It's so great having you on today, Emily.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.230
Really appreciate it.

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:14.879
Speaker 3: Thanks so much.

00:00:14.879 --> 00:00:15.879
I'm so glad to be here.

00:00:15.879 --> 00:00:18.480
Speaker 1: So, I mean, let's just start with
a little history.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.390
Many of us are familiar with with food labels
in general and also expiration dates, Best

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.750
Buy, Sell, Buy, et cetera.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:27.539
What's the sort of history of that?

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:28.539
When did it start?

00:00:28.539 --> 00:00:29.670
Why did it start?

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:30.960
Speaker 3: Mm-Hmm.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.800
So we see kind of this modern landscape of,
you know, these labels on most food products

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:40.510
really taking shape in the 70s.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:45.930
And at that time, it was really consumers
were pushing companies and saying that they

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:48.260
wanted to know about the freshness of their
products.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.640
So, you know, if you think about where we
were at that time, it was sort of starting

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.380
after like post-World War Two, people are
getting more further away from where their

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.059
food is produced, buying more things in the
grocery store.

00:01:01.059 --> 00:01:04.841
And people said, if I'm not part of the farm
and getting it directly from a farm, how will

00:01:04.841 --> 00:01:06.700
I know how fresh it is?

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.700
Right?

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:13.190
And that's when we we saw a lot of activity
in terms of voluntary use of these labels

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:14.190
on the part of companies.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:18.000
Speaker 1: Now there was also in that period
of time in like the 20 years preceding the

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.410
70s, there was a move away from most of the
things that the grocery store are ingredients

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.710
to really like the food product, right?

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.010
Something that that's manufactured and sent
that.

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:33.530
And is that also part of why we started to
see those markings that like with a banana?

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:37.510
By looking at it, you can mostly tell what
is the status of this banana?

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:39.660
Whereas with some of these products, it might
be less clear.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.740
Speaker 3: Yeah, that's totally right.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:47.300
So, you know, and I think even today in, you
know, fast forward to now, where there's their

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.410
labels on lots of products, a lot of states
require them, but it's primarily on packaged

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:57.270
foods because I think there's a sense that,
you know, banana and apple, you know, a food,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:02.010
a food that you can pick up and hold in that
sort of in its raw form that people can really

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:06.480
make their own judgments about how fresh it
is and how spoiled how close it is to being

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.710
spoiled.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.820
And it's really packaged foods that you see
these labels on because people don't really

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:14.510
know they can't open it up in the store and
smell it right there.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:23.340
Speaker 1: So there's best if used by their
sell by there's a straight up expiration date

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.730
that sometimes is there.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.970
These things presumably mean different things,
right?

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.400
Speaker 3: One would think, actually, that's
one of the biggest issues is that consumers

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.550
sort of look at them and think these labels
must be telling me different things.

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:43.440
But in fact, you know, most there's no law
that really regulates what those different

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:44.440
terms mean.

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:50.420
There's no, you know, there's no law defining
sell-by as compared to use by or expires on

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.420
right now.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.100
In fact, there's no federal law regulating
date labels at all.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.120
So the federal government, with a very small
exception of infant formula, has not required

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.140
anything.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.880
And then in the states that have requirements,
they use them very interchangeably.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:10.140
So I think that's one of the places where
we got really interested because consumers

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.140
are misled.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:14.750
They they they think that they're, you know,
that they're seeing two different labels and

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:15.990
that they mean something different.

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:18.720
But there's not there's not any evidence that
that's the case.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:26.380
Speaker 1: Well, so so I mean, if I see as
the common man, if I see sell buy, it suggests

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:31.180
to me it should be sold by that date, but
it's still pretty good for some period of

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:32.270
time beyond that.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:34.640
Best, if used by says it, it should.

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.730
To me, it should be consumed by that day.

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:38.290
But you're saying it's not actually.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:40.430
There's not actually an infrastructure that
guarantees that.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.430
That's what I'm being told.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:43.670
Speaker 3: Yeah, that's totally right.

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:49.240
And you know, as we talk about why, how this
can be fixed, I do think there is a piece

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:54.099
of drawing on what consumers think that they're
reading and reducing the number of labels

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:57.450
and really drawing on like what consumers
already are understanding.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:01.900
But just making that the case so that consumers
aren't, you know, aren't being confused about

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:05.031
it
Speaker 1: with something like, I mean, when

00:04:05.031 --> 00:04:08.780
I first really started thinking about the
value of some of these labels is when I saw,

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:11.560
for example, an expiration date on salt.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.740
And you know, when we think about salt, it's
like salt have been around for billions of

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:16.740
years.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:17.740
It's just sort of naturally occurring.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.880
And what does it mean if salt expires?

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:23.120
Does that have any meaning at all?

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.150
Why does salt expire?

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:25.150
Speaker 3: Right?

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.590
I agree with your same vinegar.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:29.070
Similarly, it's right vinegar.

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:34.430
Now it has vinegar, does some other thing
bottled water, you know?

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:39.030
And I think people are trying to deal with
this cognitive dissonance by making up some

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.920
reason that the label so I hear people say,
Oh, what?

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:46.610
Oh, the label on bottled water is like when
the plastic is going to leach into the water.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:52.160
I mean, that is there's no that is not happening
or if it is, the date is not the date of which

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:53.160
that happens.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:58.210
You know, I think you're right that there
are a lot of food products that it doesn't

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.240
it doesn't seem.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:04.650
Very clear why a label, a date label would
be needed on them, except that consumers have

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:09.570
just come to expect them on so many products,
and I've actually talked to people who have

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:13.630
said when I pick something up at the store
that doesn't have a date label on it, I'm

00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:15.230
not sure what to do.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:20.460
So, you know, again, I think in some of our
early work we thought, Well, maybe we should

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.419
just remove the label on foods where it's
really meaningless.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:28.930
And I think that gets a lot of we get a lot
of pushback from consumer groups on that.

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:32.930
So we're really thinking, how can we take
what we have and make it work better and make

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.230
it align better and be less misleading?

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:41.050
You know, knowing that in some cases we are
where we are and we can't kind of completely

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:42.160
overhaul the system.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:49.760
Speaker 1: The if we want to apply like a
corporate profit motive to this one could

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:56.910
imagine you could have foods that are safe
for much longer than the dates say.

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:01.530
But maybe the manufacturer is worried there
could be reputational damage because it might

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:04.300
not taste quite as good or whatever the case
may be.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.900
How much of that is a factor in these labels?

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.260
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:13.260
I mean, that is it's a really big factor,
especially if you think about where we are

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.260
right now, which is some states require dates
on certain foods.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:22.860
There's a quite a just, you know, huge variation
in what food states require labels on and

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.770
then what you can do after the label.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.900
So that leaves a lot up to the manufacturer
as far as like what words to use the science

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:34.330
or if there is any science behind how they're
setting that date.

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:38.490
And of course, for companies, they want you
to eat their food when it's going to taste

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:39.490
