00:13:40.839 --> 00:13:44.380
Yeah, evaporated cane evaporated.

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:47.119
Cane sirup is one you will see, and I still
see it.

00:13:47.119 --> 00:13:49.850
And I think, you know, FDA has said it's not
allowed again.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:54.459
They don't have the capacity to enforce all
of them, so consumers pay the price of that.

00:13:54.459 --> 00:14:00.249
You know, I think that there's both responsibility
on the part of the FDA and potentially on

00:14:00.249 --> 00:14:06.860
Congress to really beef up the resources so
that those can be enforced and evaporating.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:08.089
Cane sirup is literally sugar.

00:14:08.089 --> 00:14:10.170
It is what everyone would think of as sugar.

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:13.699
And yet, you know, again, it will say no sugar,
and it will have that on a product and

00:14:13.699 --> 00:14:18.339
Speaker 1: yeah, to other specific ones that
I want to ask about.

00:14:18.339 --> 00:14:24.079
So organic is actually a regulated term with
a specific meaning about about the how it's

00:14:24.079 --> 00:14:28.499
grown or whatever when it comes to all natural.

00:14:28.499 --> 00:14:33.040
What is important for people to understand
about whether all natural has any meaning

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.040
whatsoever?

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.120
Speaker 3: Mm-Hmm.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.279
Great question.

00:14:37.279 --> 00:14:43.189
No one can answer that question because there's
no definition really from the Food and Drug

00:14:43.189 --> 00:14:44.949
Administration for all natural.

00:14:44.949 --> 00:14:51.389
So there's been a huge, huge amount of litigation,
particularly in California, which has really

00:14:51.389 --> 00:14:55.329
nice laws that are very supportive towards
consumers.

00:14:55.329 --> 00:15:02.069
It's just a lot of litigation around a paddock
with an all natural claim and then plaintiffs

00:15:02.069 --> 00:15:07.739
consumers saying that they bought the product
and then realize afterwards there were certain

00:15:07.739 --> 00:15:13.730
processing elements or it was ultra processed
or it included, you know, gene edited or genetically

00:15:13.730 --> 00:15:17.819
engineered materials that people would think
don't seem natural.

00:15:17.819 --> 00:15:22.959
And you know, there's there's I would say
for now, I treat that claim as if I just ignore

00:15:22.959 --> 00:15:23.959
it completely.

00:15:23.959 --> 00:15:27.170
I would say that mean that is meaningless
at the moment.

00:15:27.170 --> 00:15:33.560
And I think again, there's there's a big question
mark as to if and when we will, we will.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:38.089
See, the FDA come out with a real definition
that can give consumers a little more confidence

00:15:38.089 --> 00:15:39.089
in that claim.

00:15:39.089 --> 00:15:41.850
Speaker 1: Last thing I want to ask about,
and I know we're now getting like a couple

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:49.610
levels removed from where we started homeopathic
products, herbal products, some supplements,

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:55.609
cetera where they say statements have not
been evaluated by the FDA, et cetera.

00:15:55.609 --> 00:15:58.410
We've all seen that type of thing.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:03.829
What are the concerns and sort of what is
in that general space that consumers should

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:06.970
be aware of when looking at those products?

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:14.160
Speaker 3: You now here's a couple of steps
from my bailiwick, and they're there, even

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:21.089
if I said already that there's a lot of claims
on foods that either FDA hasn't yet defined

00:16:21.089 --> 00:16:25.859
like natural, they don't really have a great
definition or where there is a very clear

00:16:25.859 --> 00:16:29.410
definition, but they just don't have the resources
to enforce.

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:35.220
Once you get into supplements, you know, herbal
things, etc., it's even more so that way.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:38.549
I mean, a lot of people in the food space
will call that really the Wild West in terms

00:16:38.549 --> 00:16:44.970
of the in terms of the the level of both laws
and enforcement.

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:47.139
So I think it's it's a really difficult area.

00:16:47.139 --> 00:16:49.660
I think there's a lot of folks calling for
more regulation.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:53.779
They're, you know, making sure both that there's
really two concerns.

00:16:53.779 --> 00:16:58.050
One is when you buy a supplement is what's
in there actually what it says it is right

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:01.910
back in is are the claims about what it can
do for your health.

00:17:01.910 --> 00:17:03.139
Validated in any way.

00:17:03.139 --> 00:17:07.490
And I think on both ends of that, it's a really
big question mark and it's hard for the companies

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:11.871
that are, you know, trying to do it well and
trying to do a good job because I think they

00:17:11.871 --> 00:17:16.430
will probably like more enforcement so that
they, you know there that people will have

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:17.740
more trust in that space.

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.810
Speaker 1: Yeah, without a doubt.

00:17:19.810 --> 00:17:24.510
Emily Broad Broadleaf is a clinical professor
of law and faculty director for Harvard Law

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.449
School's Food Law and Policy Clinic.

00:17:27.449 --> 00:17:28.820
Really appreciate your time today.

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:30.360
Speaker 3: Great talking to you.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:30.610
Thanks.

